  "query_preview": "..\nMonday evening through Tuesday: An unsettled weather pattern\npersists over the Pacific and Inland Northwest delivering \nperiods of rain and high mountain snow. Precipitation picks up \nregionwide by Monday evening and continues into the overnight \nhours as an occluding front sweeps inland. \n\nMost of the region will receive between 0.10 and 0.20 inches of\nrain by Tuesday morning. Lower amounts (less than 0.10 inches)\nare expected across the central Columbia Basin and L-C Valley. \nLocally higher amounts (0.20 to 0.50 inches) are forecast for \nnortheastern WA and the Selkirks of North ID, and 0.50 to 1.00 \ninches are forecast for the Cascade Crest and upper river \nvalleys of the East Slopes.\n\nA brief break in the rain will occur Tuesday morning before a\nwarm front and plume of subtropical moisture lifts through the\nregion Tuesday afternoon, bringing another round of widespread \nrain with additional rainfall amounts ranging from 0.05-0.10 \ninches across south-central and southeastern WA and the central \nID Panhandle, 0.10-0.20 inches for north-central and \nnortheastern WA and the northern ID Panhandle, 0.25-0.50 inches \nalong the East Slopes of the Cascades, and 0.50-1.25 inches \nalong the immediate crest.\n\nOverall, rains will mainly be beneficial for the Inland\nNorthwest with mild temperatures suggesting little to no \nconcerns for winter travel conditions. Washington Pass will be \nthe one spot to watch for mountain snow, with snow amounts of \n2-4 inches forecast by Tuesday night. \n\nAnother concern will be increased potential for debris flows and\nrock slides over the Cascade burn scars. This comes with low \nconfidence, but given that soils are already saturated from \nintermittent rain over the past several days, and now more rain \nis forecast to fall over the burn scars, the risk of debris \nflows and rock slides is worth mentioning.\n\nWednesday through Thursday: High pressure amplifies over the\nInland Northwest bringing drier weather, warmer than normal \ntemperatures, boundary layer winds out of the east/southeast. \nThis flow pattern banks boundary layer moisture up against the \neast slopes of the Cascades and the mountains of northern WA, \noften leading to persistent low clouds and/or dense fog in these\nareas. While the aforementioned areas will have the highest \nchances of seeing persistent low clouds and fog, most of the \nregion will be susceptible to fog development with abundant \nboundary layer moisture and relatively light winds.\n\nFriday through Monday: Ensemble clusters are in good agreement\non a cold, robust system tracking inland heading into the \nweekend as the ridge axis shifts east out of the region. This \nsystem will come with potential for a more impactful late fall \nstorm system consisting of mountain snow and gusty winds. \n/Fewkes",
